        {
            "key": "field_alphablock_query_logic_help",
            "label": "Filter guidance",
            "name": "",
            "type": "message",
            "wrapper": {
                "width": "100",
                "class": "",
                "id": ""
            },
            "message": "<p><strong>Filter types</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Persona Targets:</strong> match through a related-post field. Key is the relationship field name; Value is the target post's slug or ID. Use <code>field_name&amp;post_type</code> when the lookup post type differs from the field name. The stored type remains <code>CPT</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Meta:</strong> match a custom field or metadata value. Key is the stored field name and Value is the value to match.</li>\n<li><strong>Post Attributes:</strong> match a post property. Supported keys: <code>ID</code>, <code>author</code>, <code>parent</code>, <code>post_status</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>slug</code>, <code>date</code> and <code>modified</code>. Native names such as <code>post_author</code> and <code>post_parent</code> also work. Author and parent use numeric IDs; LIKE is for text attributes. Dates use <code>YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS</code>. Post status filters still respect published-only rendering.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Compare As</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>=:</strong> matches the complete stored value.</li>\n<li><strong>!=:</strong> does not match the complete stored value.</li>\n<li><strong>&gt; / &gt;=:</strong> greater than / greater than or equal to.</li>\n<li><strong>&lt; / &lt;=:</strong> less than / less than or equal to.</li>\n<li><strong>LIKE:</strong> contains the specified value.</li>\n<li><strong>NOT LIKE:</strong> does not contain the specified value.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>ACF arrays:</strong> relationship, checkbox and multi-select fields commonly store serialized arrays. Use LIKE to find a member; = normally compares the entire stored value. In a Meta filter for a string ID, use double quotes, for example <code>\"123\"</code>, to avoid matching 1234. Persona Targets formats LIKE ID matches for you. The existing relates_to equality filter also handles relationship ID arrays. Single-value text fields can use = for an exact match. Meta ordering comparisons use WordPress's existing stored-value comparison; they do not automatically select a numeric type.</p>\n<p><strong>Values and order:</strong> supported merge tokens can be used in Value. All requires every filter; Any requires at least one; Build processes rows in order until the quota is filled. Drag rows to change Build priority.</p>",
            "new_lines": "",
            "esc_html": 0
        },
