        {
            "key": "field_alphablock_family_help",
            "label": "Family and merge tokens",
            "name": "",
            "type": "message",
            "wrapper": {
                "width": "100",
                "class": "",
                "id": ""
            },
            "message": "<p>Choose the reference post in Whose family?, then select one or more relationships to display. Enter a post ID, use an owner ID token below, or use <code>0</code> for the hierarchy root. Children of <code>0</code> are top-level posts.</p>\n<p>Merge tokens are replaced before the block is processed. They can be used in string values throughout the block configuration, including within longer text.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>%me%</code> or <code>%me-&gt;ID%</code>: the ID of the post containing this block.</li>\n<li><code>%me-slug%</code>: the slug of that post. The existing <code>%me-&gt;slug%</code> spelling also works.</li>\n<li><code>%mine-&gt;meta_key%</code>: a metadata value on that post. Replace <code>meta_key</code> with the actual field name, for example <code>%mine-&gt;related_parent%</code>. For multiple values, the first item is used; a missing value becomes empty.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Whose family? needs an ID or <code>0</code>, so use an ID token or metadata containing a post ID here. Slug tokens are for settings that accept text or slugs.</p>\n<p>Legacy <code>%mine%</code> remains supported: in Whose family? it means the owning post ID; in a filter value it reads that filter's metadata key on the owning post. Unknown tokens remain unchanged.</p>",
            "new_lines": "",
            "esc_html": 0
        },
